<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Member or not a member</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>\(A = {@Ad@}\). Is each statement true or false?</p><p>(a) \({@p@} \in A\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) \({@q@} \notin A\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p><p>(c) \(n(A) = {@c@}\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p><p>(d) \({@r@} \in A\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>First list the set: \(A = {@dset(A)@}\), so \(n(A) = {@cardn(A)@}\).</p><p>(a) \({@p@}\) [[if test="elementp(p,A)"]]is[[else]]is not[[/if]] an element of \(A\), so the statement is [[if test="is(k1=1)"]]true[[else]]false[[/if]].</p><p>(b) \({@q@}\) [[if test="elementp(q,A)"]]is[[else]]is not[[/if]] an element of \(A\). The symbol \(\notin\) means <q>is not an element of</q>, so the statement is [[if test="is(k2=1)"]]true[[else]]false[[/if]].</p><p>(c) \(A\) has \({@cardn(A)@}\) elements, so the statement is [[if test="is(k3=1)"]]true[[else]]false[[/if]].</p><p>(d) \({@r@}\) [[if test="elementp(r,A)"]]is[[else]]is not[[/if]] an element of \(A\), so the statement is [[if test="is(k4=1)"]]true[[else]]false[[/if]].</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>4</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[cardn(S) := length(listify(S));
dset(S) := if emptyp(S) then "\\{\\,\\}" else sconcat("\\{", simplode(map(string, listify(S)), ", "), "\\}");
ix: 1+rand(10);
tf(k) := [[1, is(k=1), "True"], [2, is(k=2), "False"]];
/* Design: true-or-false statements about membership and n(A). Source: adapted from SMP Book 3, Chapter 11, Exercise A, question 8. Curated list.
   [description TeX, A, p, q, c, r]: (a) p in A, (b) q notin A, (c) n(A) = c, (d) r in A. Keys 1 true, 2 false. */
L: [["\\{\\text{the factors of 36}\\}",{1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,36},12,8,8,24],["\\{\\text{the multiples of 9 between 10 and 70}\\}",{18,27,36,45,54,63},81,45,6,27],["\\{\\text{the prime numbers less than 25}\\}",{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23},21,1,9,23],["\\{\\text{the odd factors of 60}\\}",{1,3,5,15},15,5,4,30],["\\{\\text{the square numbers between 20 and 150}\\}",{25,36,49,64,81,100,121,144},121,125,7,150],["\\{\\text{the factors of 50}\\}",{1,2,5,10,25,50},20,25,6,50],["\\{\\text{the multiples of 11 between 30 and 100}\\}",{33,44,55,66,77,88,99},111,110,7,77],["\\{\\text{the prime numbers between 70 and 90}\\}",{71,73,79,83,89},81,87,6,79],["\\{\\text{the even factors of 40}\\}",{2,4,8,10,20,40},40,1,8,5],["\\{\\text{the two-digit numbers whose digits add up to 7}\\}",{16,25,34,43,52,61,70},7,61,7,52]];
Ad: L[ix][1]; A: L[ix][2]; p: L[ix][3]; q: L[ix][4]; c: L[ix][5]; r: L[ix][6];
k1: if elementp(p,A) then 1 else 2; k2: if not elementp(q,A) then 1 else 2;
k3: if is(cardn(A)=c) then 1 else 2; k4: if elementp(r,A) then 1 else 2;
o1: tf(k1); o2: tf(k2); o3: tf(k3); o4: tf(k4);
/* test values: the other option */
f1: 3-k1; f2: 3-k2; f3: 3-k3; f4: 3-k4;
]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
